## 🔵 3. Types of SQL JOINs

### 1️⃣ INNER JOIN

Returns only the rows that have matching values in both tables.

```sql
SELECT Students.name, Departments.department
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Departments
ON Students.dept_id = Departments.dept_id;
```

**Result:**

| name | department |
|---|---|
| Arun | CSE |
| Priya | IT |
| Divya | CSE |

💡 Kavin is excluded because dept_id 103 has no matching department.

---

### 2️⃣ LEFT JOIN (LEFT OUTER JOIN)

Returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table. If there is no match, the right-table columns contain `NULL`.

```sql
SELECT Students.name, Departments.department
FROM Students
LEFT JOIN Departments
ON Students.dept_id = Departments.dept_id;
```

**Result:**

| name | department |
|---|---|
| Arun | CSE |
| Priya | IT |
| Kavin | NULL |
| Divya | CSE |

💡 Every student is included, even if there is no matching department.

---

### 3️⃣ RIGHT JOIN (RIGHT OUTER JOIN)

Returns all rows from the right table and matching rows from the left table. If there is no match, the left-table columns contain `NULL`.

```sql
SELECT Students.name, Departments.department
FROM Students
RIGHT JOIN Departments
ON Students.dept_id = Departments.dept_id;
```

**Result:**

| name | department |
|---|---|
| Arun | CSE |
| Priya | IT |
| Divya | CSE |
| NULL | ECE |

💡 ECE is included even though no student belongs to dept_id 104.

---

### 4️⃣ FULL OUTER JOIN

Returns all matching and non-matching rows from both tables.

```sql
SELECT Students.name, Departments.department
FROM Students
FULL OUTER JOIN Departments
ON Students.dept_id = Departments.dept_id;
```

💡 This includes every student and every department, with `NULL` for missing matches.

⚠️ **Note:** MySQL does not directly support `FULL OUTER JOIN`. It can be emulated using `LEFT JOIN`, `RIGHT JOIN`, and `UNION` (with care to avoid duplicate rows).

---

### 5️⃣ CROSS JOIN

Returns every possible combination of rows from both tables.

```sql
SELECT Students.name, Departments.department
FROM Students
CROSS JOIN Departments;
```

💡 If the first table has 4 rows and the second has 3 rows, the result contains **4 × 3 = 12 rows**.

---

## 📊 4. JOINs — Quick Comparison

| JOIN Type | What It Returns |
|---|---|
| INNER JOIN | Only matching rows |
| LEFT JOIN | All left-table rows + matches |
| RIGHT JOIN | All right-table rows + matches |
| FULL OUTER JOIN | All rows from both tables |
| CROSS JOIN | Every possible combination |

